WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.660
Raymond Cruz made an indelible impression
on television viewers with his instantly unforgettable

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.750
performance as bad guy Tuco Salamanca on Breaking
Bad.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.580
The character proved so popular, they brought
him back for the prequel spinoff Better Call

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.580
Saul.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.600
So it's a bit surprising that Cruz hasn't
gotten more recognition - until now, that

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:21.600
is.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.250
Here's a look at the untold truth about the
guy who played Tuco on Breaking Bad.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.880
Gang hit

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.539
Maybe the eeriest thing about Cruz's performance
on Breaking Bad is how fully and comfortably

00:00:33.539 --> 00:00:37.929
he seems to inhabit Tuco's world of drug running
and gang banging.

00:00:37.929 --> 00:00:43.179
But there's a good reason for that: Cruz himself
grew up surrounded by hardcore gang activity

00:00:43.179 --> 00:00:47.460
on the hardscrabble streets of East L.A. How
bad was it?

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.829
He told the Daily Beast that when he was just
12, he witnessed a cold blooded homicide.

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:57.199
"I saw someone get shot in front of me at
point blank range [...] The brains came out

00:00:57.199 --> 00:00:59.209
the back of his head."

00:00:59.209 --> 00:01:00.209
Yikes!

00:01:00.209 --> 00:01:01.209
By the book

00:01:01.209 --> 00:01:05.740
As a kid, Cruz escaped the world of street
gangs both literally and metaphorically through

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.740
his love of literature.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.689
Retreating into books, he read and reread
classic novels, and that passion eventually

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:17.200
led him to acting, thanks in part to an assist
from Gregory Peck.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.939
Cruz told Smashing Interviews,

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:23.750
"I was fascinated by the translation of the
written word to performance.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.159
I read Harper Lee's To Kill a Mockingbird,
and it was one of my favorite books.

00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:31.420
Then when I saw the movie, I was over the
moon about it."

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.030
"That guy"

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.810
Cruz may be best known for playing Tuco on
Breaking Bad and Detective Julio Sanchez on

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:43.039
The Closer and Major Crimes, but he's actually
been toiling away in Hollywood as a classic

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:45.150
"that guy" since the '80s.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:51.060
He got his big break in the now-forgotten
1987 comedy film Maid to Order, and has since

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.090
landed more than 80 film and television roles,
from movies like Clear and Present Danger

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:01.429
and Training Day, to TV shows including Cagney
and Lacey and Murder, She Wrote.

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:05.540
We love it when a character actor finally
becomes a star!

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.700
Good guys and bad guys

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.960
It's not that unusual for a popular character
to spin off from a show into another series.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.580
But Cruz holds the distinction of doing it
twice - and at the same time.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:23.610
While playing Detective Julio Sanchez on The
Closer, a role he originated way back in 2005,

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:28.270
he moonlighted as Tuco on Breaking Bad, working
seven days a week to film both parts

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.330
concurrently.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:33.360
He apparently liked the challenge, though,
because after Detective Sanchez spun off into

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.430
Major Crimes, he then reprised his role as
Tuco on Better Call Saul at the same time.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:42.320
Given that one character is a cop and the
other a criminal, is it possible we could

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.210
ever see a crossover?

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.810
He loves to cook

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:51.120
One of Tuco's most intriguing character traits
is the fact that he really enjoyed cooking.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.680
Not meth like Walter White, mind you, but
delicious hand-crafted burritos and other

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.890
meals made with... love?

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.780
So where did Tuco get it from?

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.460
Well, from Cruz, of course, who is a bit of
a chef in his own right.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:08.150
Just don't bother trying to emulate his grilled
pork chop recipe, because it requires an ingredient

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.020
not for sale: his mom's homemade chili.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.020
He told AMC,

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.760
"I'll put it on a bed of lettuce with a grilled
jalapeno or a small green chile, take it to

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:21.640
the point just before it starts to cook completely
through and I'll top it with my mom's chili

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.370
and a couple slices of cheese.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.680
Man, it's the best thing ever."

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:26.680
He's an introvert

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.760
Tuco might be a super-intense, in-your-face
villain, but in real life, Cruz couldn't be

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.090
more different.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:38.230
In fact, he's basically an introvert, preferring
quiet reading to drug-fueled rampages.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.720
He told Smashing Interviews,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.900
"I'm so quiet, and I like to read.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.280
I like to tinker in the garage.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.650
I love working on motorcycles and cars.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.040
I like to be alone.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.550
If I'm in a room with a lot of people, I'm
probably the last person you'll notice.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.880
I'm practically invisible."

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.260
Hard pass

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.890
When Cruz was initially approached by the
producers of Breaking Bad about the role of

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:02.050
Tuco Salamanca, Cruz had a simple answer for
them: no.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:06.990
Sure, it's hard to imagine someone else as
Tuco now, but Cruz was concerned that filming

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:11.790
both Breaking Bad and The Closer at the same
time would be too much work for him.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:16.250
But finally they convinced him the old fashioned
way: by appealing to his ego.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.510
He told the AV Club,

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:19.260
"They couldn't find anyone who could do it.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.960
I was shooting seven days a week with no rest
and working on two different things.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:23.960
It was crazy."

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.220
Tuco's demise

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.270
Tuco's demise was shocking, but even more
surprising is the fact that it was Cruz himself

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.820
who asked the producers of Breaking Bad to
eliminate the character.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:35.820
Why?

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.340
Partly because of his grueling filming schedule,
but also because there was at least one prominent

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.790
fan of the show who really disliked the character:
Cruz's wife.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:45.940
Cruz told Rotten Tomatoes,

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:47.320
"My wife hated it.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.560
She hated Tuco.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.880
She hated the energy.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:52.210
Tuco is this wild beast.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.090
My wife is like, 'Get away from me.'"

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.490
Tuco might be tough, but he's no match for
an unhappy wife.

